@node	4.2, 4.3, 4.1, Chapter 4
@section Lazy Evaluation

Now that we have an evaluator expressed as a Rust program, we can experiment
with alternative choices in language design simply by modifying the evaluator.
Indeed, new languages are often invented by first writing an evaluator that
embeds the new language within an existing high-level language.  For example,
if we wish to discuss some aspect of a proposed modification to the object
language with another member of the language community, we can supply an
evaluator that embodies the change.  The recipient can then experiment with
the new evaluator and send back comments as further modifications.  Not only
does the high-level implementation base make it easier to test and debug the
evaluator; in addition, the embedding enables the designer to
snarf@footnote{Snarf: ``To grab, especially a large document or file for the
purpose of using it either with or without the owner's permission.''  Snarf
down: ``To snarf, sometimes with the connotation of absorbing, processing, or
understanding.''  (These definitions were snarfed from @ref{Steele et
al. 1983}.  See also @ref{Raymond 1993}.)} features from the underlying
language, just as our embedded evaluator uses primitives and control structure
from the underlying Rust.  Only later (if ever) need the designer go to the
trouble of building a complete implementation in a low-level language or in
hardware.

In this section the variation is not a change to the Rust guest itself.  The
guest grammar has no implicit laziness: an ordinary call evaluates its
operands before the call, a Rust closure call is strict, and no iterator
becomes lazy.  Instead this section studies two @newterm{named experiments},
@code{lazy-recompute/1} and @code{lazy-memo/1}, which are separate evaluators
over explicit @code{Thunk} and @code{Force} syntax data.  They live in the
module @code{ch04::sec_4_2}, are selected by the typed mode value
@code{Mode::Recompute} or @code{Mode::Memo}, and each is checked against its
own finite reference model.  The core evaluator of @ref{4.1} remains strict
throughout.

@menu
* 4.2.1::            Normal Order and Applicative Order
* 4.2.2::            An Interpreter with Lazy Evaluation
* 4.2.3::            Streams as Lazy Lists
@end menu

@node	4.2.1, 4.2.2, 4.2, 4.2
@subsection Normal Order and Applicative Order

In @ref{1.1}, where we began our discussion of models of evaluation, we
noted that the guest language is an @newterm{applicative-order} language,
namely, that all the arguments to Rust functions and closures are evaluated
when the procedure is applied.  In contrast, @newterm{normal-order} languages
delay evaluation of procedure arguments until the actual argument values are
needed.  Delaying evaluation of procedure arguments until the last possible
moment (e.g., until they are required by a primitive operation) is called
@newterm{lazy evaluation}.@footnote{The difference between the ``lazy''
terminology and the ``normal-order'' terminology is somewhat fuzzy.
Generally, ``lazy'' refers to the mechanisms of particular evaluators, while
``normal-order'' refers to the semantics of languages, independent of any
particular evaluation strategy.  But this is not a hard-and-fast distinction,
and the two terminologies are often used interchangeably.}  Consider the
experiment program

@example rust
// try(a, b): answer b when a is nonzero, answer 1 otherwise.
let try_procedure = LazyExpr::Lambda(
    vec![String::from("a"), String::from("b")],
    Box::new(LazyExpr::If(
        Box::new(LazyExpr::Var(String::from("a"))),
        Box::new(LazyExpr::Var(String::from("b"))),
        Box::new(LazyExpr::Int(1)),
    )),
);
let delayed_b =
    LazyExpr::Emit(String::from("b was evaluated"));
let program = LazyExpr::Apply(
    Box::new(try_procedure),
    vec![LazyExpr::Int(0), delayed_b],
);
let mut engine = LazyEngine::new(Mode::Memo);
let outcome = engine.run(&program);
assert_eq!(outcome.value, Some(1));
assert!(outcome.effects.is_empty());
@end example

@noindent
The experiment's conditions are integers: zero is false and any other value is
true, which is a property of this experiment data language, not of Rust.  The
call binds its arguments as delayed operands, and the @code{If} demands only
the condition, so the operand carrying @code{Emit("b was evaluated")} is never
demanded and the effect log stays empty.  Evaluating the same call under
applicative order would evaluate the second operand first and the effect
would appear.  With lazy
evaluation, there is no such effect.  Evaluating the expression returns 1,
because the second argument is never evaluated.

An example that exploits lazy evaluation is the definition of a procedure
@code{unless}, which answers with its usual value unless a condition holds, in
which case it answers with its exceptional value:

@example rust
// unless(condition, usual, exceptional): the experiment's If with
// swapped branches.
let unless = LazyExpr::Lambda(
    vec![
        String::from("condition"),
        String::from("usual"),
        String::from("exceptional"),
    ],
    Box::new(LazyExpr::If(
        Box::new(LazyExpr::Var(String::from("condition"))),
        Box::new(LazyExpr::Var(String::from("exceptional"))),
        Box::new(LazyExpr::Var(String::from("usual"))),
    )),
);
@end example

@noindent
This won't work in an applicative-order language because both the usual value
and the exceptional value will be evaluated before @code{unless} is called
(compare @ref{Exercise 1.6}).  An advantage of lazy evaluation is that some
procedures, such as @code{unless}, can do useful computation even if evaluation
of some of their arguments would produce errors or would not terminate.

If the body of a procedure is entered before an argument has been evaluated we
say that the procedure is @newterm{non-strict} in that argument.  If the
argument is evaluated before the body of the procedure is entered we say that
the procedure is @newterm{strict} in that argument.@footnote{The ``strict''
versus ``non-strict'' terminology means essentially the same thing as
``applicative-order'' versus ``normal-order,'' except that it refers to
individual procedures and arguments rather than to the language as a whole.  At
a conference on programming languages you might hear someone say, ``The
normal-order language Hassle has certain strict primitives.  Other procedures
take their arguments by lazy evaluation.''}  In a purely applicative-order
language, all procedures are strict in each argument.  In a purely normal-order
language, all compound procedures are non-strict in each argument, and
primitive procedures may be either strict or non-strict.  There are also
languages (see @ref{Exercise 4.31}) that give programmers detailed control over
the strictness of the procedures they define.

A striking example of a procedure that can usefully be made non-strict is
a constructor for data structures.  One
can do useful computation, combining elements to form data structures and
operating on the resulting data structures, even if the values of the elements
are not known.  It makes perfect sense, for instance, to compute the length of
a list without knowing the values of the individual elements in the list.  We
will exploit this idea in @ref{4.2.3} to implement lazy lists as lists formed
of non-strict pairs.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.25}Exercise 4.25:} Suppose that (in the ordinary
applicative-order evaluator of @ref{4.1}) we define @code{unless} as shown
above and then define @code{factorial} in terms of @code{unless} as

@example
factorial(n) =
  unless(n == 1,
         n * factorial(n - 1),
         1)
@end example

What happens if we attempt to evaluate @code{factorial(5)}?  Will our
definitions work in a normal-order language?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.26}Exercise 4.26:} Ben Bitdiddle and Alyssa
P. Hacker disagree over the importance of lazy evaluation for implementing
things such as @code{unless}.  Ben points out that it's possible to implement
@code{unless} in applicative order as a special form.  Alyssa counters that, if
one did that, @code{unless} would be merely syntax, not a procedure that could
be used in conjunction with higher-order procedures.  Fill in the details on
both sides of the argument.  Show how to implement @code{unless} as a derived
expression (like the case form or @code{Let} of @ref{4.1.2}), and give an
example of a situation where it might be useful to have @code{unless} available
as a procedure, rather than as a special form.
@end quotation

@node	4.2.2, 4.2.3, 4.2.1, 4.2
@subsection An Interpreter with Lazy Evaluation

In this section we will implement a normal-order experiment language whose
compound procedures are non-strict in each argument.
Primitive procedures will still be strict.  The experiment is a separate
evaluator over explicit syntax data; it does not alter the strict evaluator of
@ref{4.1.1}, and no Rust call becomes lazy implicitly.  Almost all the
required changes center around procedure application.

The basic idea is that, when applying a procedure, the interpreter must
determine which arguments are to be evaluated and which are to be delayed.  The
delayed arguments are not evaluated; instead, they are transformed into objects
called @newterm{thunks}.@footnote{The word @newterm{thunk} was invented by an
informal working group that was discussing the implementation of call-by-name
in Algol 60.  They observed that most of the analysis of (``thinking about'')
the expression could be done at compile time; thus, at run time, the expression
would already have been ``thunk'' about (@ref{Ingerman et al. 1960}).} The
thunk must contain the information required to produce the value of the
argument when it is needed, as if it had been evaluated at the time of the
application.  Thus, the thunk must contain the argument expression and the
environment in which the procedure application is being evaluated.  In the
experiment data a thunk is explicit: @code{Thunk(id, body)} packages a body
expression with an identity, and evaluating it produces the delayed value
@code{Later(id)} without running the body.

The process of evaluating the expression in a thunk is called
@newterm{forcing}.@footnote{This is analogous to the use of @code{force} on the
delayed objects that were introduced in @ref{Chapter 3} to represent streams.
The critical difference between what we are doing here and what we did in
@ref{Chapter 3} is that we are building delaying and forcing into the
evaluator, and thus making this uniform and automatic throughout the
experiment language.}  In general, a thunk will be forced only when its value
is needed: when it is passed to a primitive procedure that will use the value
of the thunk; when it is the value of a predicate of a conditional; and when it
is the value of an operator that is about to be applied as a procedure.  One
design choice we have available is whether or not to @newterm{memoize} thunks,
as we did with delayed objects in @ref{3.5.1}.  With memoization, the first
time a thunk is forced, it stores the value that is computed.  Subsequent
forcings simply return the stored value without repeating the computation.
The experiment names both choices: in @code{lazy-memo/1} the first successful
result is stored per thunk identity and its effects occur once; in
@code{lazy-recompute/1} every forcing evaluates the expression again.  In both
modes a failed force stays delayed.  We'll make our discussion memoizing by
default, because this is more efficient for many applications.
There are tricky considerations here, however.@footnote{Lazy evaluation
combined with memoization is sometimes referred to as @newterm{call-by-need}
argument passing, in contrast to @newterm{call-by-name} argument passing.
(Call-by-name, introduced in Algol 60, is similar to non-memoized lazy
evaluation.)  As language designers, we can build our evaluator to memoize, not
to memoize, or leave this an option for programmers (@ref{Exercise 4.31}).  As
you might expect from @ref{Chapter 3}, these choices raise issues that become
both subtle and confusing in the presence of effects.  (See @ref{Exercise 4.27}
and @ref{Exercise 4.29}.)  An excellent article by @ref{Clinger (1982)}
attempts to clarify the multiple dimensions of confusion that arise here.}

@subsubheading Modifying the evaluator

The main difference between the lazy experiment and the evaluator in
@ref{4.1} is in the handling of procedure applications.  The experiment's
syntax data carries the delay and demand forms explicitly:

@example rust
pub enum LazyExpr @{
    Int(i64),
    Var(String),
    Thunk(usize, Box<LazyExpr>),
    Force(Box<LazyExpr>),
    Add(Box<LazyExpr>, Box<LazyExpr>),
    Mul(Box<LazyExpr>, Box<LazyExpr>),
    Let(String, Box<LazyExpr>, Box<LazyExpr>),
    Emit(String),
    If(Box<LazyExpr>, Box<LazyExpr>, Box<LazyExpr>),
    Apply(Box<LazyExpr>, Vec<LazyExpr>),
    Lambda(Vec<String>, Box<LazyExpr>),
    Pair(Box<LazyExpr>, Box<LazyExpr>),
    Empty,
    Quote(LazyVal),
@}
@end example

@noindent
and its values are integers, delayed identities, explicit lazy pairs, the
empty datum, primitive operators, and closures over this data:

@example rust
pub enum LazyVal @{
    Now(i64),
    Later(usize),
    PairVal(Box<LazyVal>, Box<LazyVal>),
    EmptyVal,
    Prim(PrimOp),
    Closure(Vec<String>, Box<LazyExpr>, Vec<(String, LazyVal)>),
@}
@end example

@noindent
The application clause is the heart of the change.  The operator is demanded
first, so the dispatcher can tell a primitive from a closure; a closure then
binds every argument as a delayed thunk, while a primitive demands its
operands before it runs:

@example rust
LazyExpr::Apply(operator, args) => @{
    // The operator is demanded first (the exercise 4.28 ordering).
    let callable = self.eval(operator, env)?;
    @r{…}
    if let LazyVal::Closure(params, _, _) = &callable @{
        if params.len() != args.len() @{
            return None;
        @}
        for (param, arg) in params.iter().zip(args) @{
            // Arguments bind as delayed thunks.
            let id = self.register_fresh(arg, env);
            extended.push((param.clone(), LazyVal::Later(id)));
        @}
    @} else @{
        // Primitive application demands its operands.
        let mut values = Vec::with_capacity(args.len());
        for arg in args @{
            values.push(self.eval(arg, env)?);
        @}
        return self.apply_prim(callable, &values);
    @}
    @r{…}
@}
@end example

@noindent
This is almost the same as the application clause of @code{evaluate} in
@ref{4.1.1}, except that a compound procedure receives unevaluated operand
expressions packaged as thunks.  Each thunk carries its operand expression
together with the environment where it was delayed, and forcing evaluates
the body in that stored environment rather than in the caller's environment
at the force site.  We still evaluate the operator first, because dispatch needs the
actual procedure (primitive versus closure) to apply it.

Whenever we need the actual value of an expression, we demand it: the
expression is evaluated and, if its value is a delayed thunk, the thunk is
forced, recursively, until the value is not a thunk.  In the experiment this
is the @code{Force} form and the forcing loop:

@example rust
LazyExpr::Force(target) => @{
    let demanded = self.eval(target, env)?;
    match demanded @{
        LazyVal::Later(id) => self.force_id(id),
        other => Some(other),
    @}
@},
@end example

@noindent
The other place we must change the evaluator is in the handling of
conditionals, where the condition is demanded before it is tested.  The
experiment's @code{If} demands its condition as an integer and takes the
@code{then} branch when the condition is nonzero:

@example rust
LazyExpr::If(condition, then, otherwise) => @{
    let decision = self.demand_int(condition, env)?;
    if decision != 0 @{
        self.eval(then, env)
    @} else @{
        self.eval(otherwise, env)
    @}
@},
@end example

@noindent
Finally, the run is announced by an outcome value rather than by a prompt:
the engine answers the demanded integer, the ordered effect log, and the
rendered forms of produced values, so that a delayed value propagated back to
the driver is forced and printed.  The two prompts of the book's driver loop
collapse into one observation record:

@example rust
pub struct LazyOutcome @{
    pub value: Option<i64>,
    pub effects: Vec<String>,
    pub rendered: Vec<String>,
@}

let mut engine = LazyEngine::new(Mode::Memo);
let outcome = engine.run(&program);
@end example

@noindent
With these changes made, we can start the experiment and test it.  The
successful evaluation of the @code{try} expression discussed in
@ref{4.2.1} indicates that the interpreter is performing lazy evaluation: the
armed operand's effect never appears in the log.

@example rust
let mut engine = LazyEngine::new(Mode::Memo);
let outcome = engine.run(&try_program);
// => value: 1
// => effects: (empty)
assert_eq!(outcome.value, Some(1));
assert!(outcome.effects.is_empty());
@end example

@subsubheading Representing thunks

Our evaluator must arrange to create thunks when procedures are applied to
arguments and to force these thunks later.  A thunk must package an expression
together with the environment, so that the argument can be produced later.  To
force the thunk, we simply extract the expression and environment from the
thunk and evaluate the expression in the environment.  We demand the value
rather than merely evaluating it, so that in case the value of the expression
is itself a thunk, we will force that, and so on, until we reach something
that is not a thunk.  The engine keeps one table of registered thunks keyed
by identity, where each entry pairs the delayed body with its creating
environment, and one memo table of results:

@example rust
pub struct LazyEngine @{
    mode: Mode,
    memo: HashMap<usize, Option<LazyVal>>,
    effects: Vec<String>,
    thunks: HashMap<usize, (LazyExpr, HashMap<String, LazyVal>)>,
    next_id: usize,
@}
@end example

@noindent
Actually, what we want for our interpreter is not quite this, but rather
thunks that have been memoized.  When a thunk is forced, we will turn it into
an evaluated thunk by storing the computed value under the thunk's identity so
that it can be recognized as already evaluated.@footnote{Notice that we also
erase the expression from the memo once the expression's value has been
computed.  This makes no difference in the values returned by the interpreter.
It does help save space, however, because dropping the stored expression and
its environment once they are no longer needed allows this structure to be
@newterm{garbage-collected} and its space recycled, as we will discuss in
@ref{5.3}.  Similarly, we could have allowed unneeded environments in the
memoized delayed objects of @ref{3.5.1} to be garbage-collected, by having
the memoized procedure discard the procedure @code{proc} (which includes the
environment in which the delay was evaluated) after storing its value.}  In
@code{lazy-memo/1} the first successful result is stored per thunk identity
and its effects occur once; in @code{lazy-recompute/1} the body is evaluated
again at every forcing and the memo table stays empty.  In both modes a failed
force stays delayed, so a later demand may try the body again.

@noindent
Notice that the same thunk machinery works both with and without
memoization: the experiment's mode value selects the behavior, the identities
are the same, and the observable difference is the effect log and the amount
of repeated work.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.27}Exercise 4.27:} Suppose we type in the following
definitions to the lazy evaluator, where @code{tick} records one effect for
each execution of the procedure body:

@example
