id(x) = (count := count + 1; x)
@end example

@noindent
In the experiment data the same probe is written with the effect log, one
@code{Emit("tick")} standing for one increment of @code{count}:

@example rust
// id(x) = Let("_", Emit("tick"), Var("x"))
let id = LazyExpr::Lambda(
    vec![String::from("x")],
    Box::new(LazyExpr::Let(
        String::from("_"),
        Box::new(LazyExpr::Emit(String::from("tick"))),
        Box::new(LazyExpr::Var(String::from("x"))),
    )),
);
@end example

Give the missing values in the following sequence of interactions, and explain
your answers.@footnote{This exercise demonstrates that the interaction between
lazy evaluation and side effects can be very confusing.  This is just what you
might expect from the discussion in @ref{Chapter 3}.}

@example
w = id(id(10))

⟨@var{effects so far}⟩

demand w

⟨@var{response}⟩

⟨@var{effects so far}⟩
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.28}Exercise 4.28:} The application clause of
@ref{4.2.2} demands the operator's value rather than merely evaluating it
