@quotation
@strong{In this language: the lazy application clause, the memoized thunk, and the printable lazy pairs.}
The book's lazy layer is a change to one evaluator; this edition keeps the
core evaluator strict and puts the variation in two named experiments,
@code{lazy-recompute/1} and @code{lazy-memo/1}, over explicit
@code{Thunk}/@code{Force} data.  The lazy layer does not fork the semantics
anywhere else: the same experiment data is checked against the independent
finite reference model of @code{sec_4_2}, and the mode value selects the
forcing discipline.  The application clause binds every closure argument as a
fresh delayed thunk and demands the operator first; the memo table stores the
first successful result per thunk identity and runs its effects once, while
the recomputing mode evaluates the body at every demand; and lazy pairs carry
explicit pair data whose renderings are themselves produced for printing.
One boundary is honest and unchanged: forcing is ordinary host recursion, so
a deeply nested demand uses the host stack like any other call.
@end quotation

@node	4.2.3, 4.3, 4.2.2, 4.2
@subsection Streams as Lazy Lists

In @ref{3.5.1}, we showed how to implement streams as delayed lists.
We introduced delayed objects that allowed us to construct a ``promise'' to
compute the rest of a stream, without actually fulfilling that promise until
later.  We could use this general technique of introducing delaying whenever we
need more control over the evaluation process, but this is awkward.  For one
thing, a delayed object is not a first-class object like a procedure, so we
cannot use it together with higher-order procedures.@footnote{This is
precisely the issue with the @code{unless} procedure, as in
@ref{Exercise 4.26}.}  Also, we were forced to create streams as a new kind of
data object similar but not identical to lists, and this required us to
reimplement many ordinary list operations (@code{map}, @code{append}, and so
on) for use with streams.  With lazy evaluation, streams and lists are
identical, so there is no need for special forms or for separate list and
stream operations.  All we need to do is to arrange matters so that the pair
constructor is non-strict.

In the experiment language the pair is explicit syntax data whose two slots
are values that may be delayed:

@example rust
LazyExpr::Pair(Box<LazyExpr>, Box<LazyExpr>)
@end example

@noindent
and the corresponding value is a lazy pair:

@example rust
LazyVal::PairVal(Box<LazyVal>, Box<LazyVal>)
@end example

@noindent
The empty datum is @code{Empty} / @code{EmptyVal}, the selectors are the
primitive operators @code{PrimOp::First} and @code{PrimOp::Rest}, the
constructor is @code{PrimOp::PairBuild}, and the tests are
@code{PrimOp::IsEmpty} and @code{PrimOp::IsPair}.  Recall (@ref{2.1.3}) that
there is no fundamental need to implement pairs as primitives at all.  Instead,
we can represent pairs as procedures:@footnote{This is the procedural
representation described in @ref{Exercise 2.4}.  Essentially any procedural
representation (e.g., a message-passing implementation) would do as well.}
Notice that we can install these definitions in the lazy experiment simply by
writing them in its data language.  For example, the pair of @code{x} and
@code{y} can be a closure that dispatches a selector to its two halves:

@example rust
// pair(x, y) = Lambda([m], Apply(Var("m"), [Var("x"), Var("y")]))
let pair = LazyExpr::Lambda(
    vec![String::from("m")],
    Box::new(LazyExpr::Apply(
        Box::new(LazyExpr::Var(String::from("m"))),
        vec![
            LazyExpr::Var(String::from("x")),
            LazyExpr::Var(String::from("y")),
        ],
    )),
);
// first(p) = Apply(p, [Lambda([a, d], Var("a"))])
@end example

@noindent
In terms of these basic operations, the standard definitions of the list
operations will work with infinite lists (streams) as well as finite ones, and
the stream operations can be implemented as list operations.  The following
definitions are written in the constructor notation of the experiment's data
(the @code{LazyExpr} and @code{LazyVal} forms of @ref{4.2.2}), not as
executable Rust.  Here are some examples; @code{ones} is the infinite list of
ones, @code{integers} starts at
1 and adds the list of ones to its own rest, and @code{list_ref} walks
@code{n} rest steps and demands the first element:

@example
ones = Pair(1, Thunk(1, ones))
integers = Pair(1, Thunk(2, add_lists(ones, integers)))
list_ref(items, n) =
  if n == 0 then first(items) else list_ref(rest(items), n - 1)

list_ref(integers, 17)
// => 18
@end example

@noindent
Note that these lazy lists are even lazier than the streams of
@ref{Chapter 3}: the @code{first} of the list, as well as the @code{rest}, is
delayed.@footnote{This permits us to create delayed data of more general kinds
of list structures, not just sequences.  @ref{Hughes 1990} discusses some
applications of ``lazy trees.''}  In fact, even accessing the @code{first} or
@code{rest} of a lazy pair need not force the value of a list element.  The
value will be forced only when it is really needed---e.g., for use as the
argument of a primitive operation, or to be printed in an answer.  Lazy pairs
also help with the problem that arose with streams in @ref{3.5.4}, where we
found that formulating stream models of systems with loops may require us to
sprinkle our programs with explicit @code{Thunk} operations, beyond the ones
supplied by delayed pairs.  With lazy evaluation, all arguments to procedures
are delayed uniformly.  For instance, we can implement procedures to integrate
lists and solve differential equations, as we originally intended to do in
@ref{3.5.4} (again in the constructor notation):

@example
integral(integrand, initial_value, dt) =
  Pair(initial_value,
       Thunk(3, integral(add_lists(scale_list(dt, integrand),
                                   integrand),
                         initial_value + first(integrand) * dt,
                         dt)))
solve(f, y0, dt) =
  y where y = integral(dy, y0, dt)
            dy = scale_list(dt, map(f, y))
@end example

@noindent
The experiment language computes with @code{i64} only: the guest has no
floating point.  The textbook's @code{dt = 0.001} therefore becomes the same
fixed-point scale used in the Newton-iteration lesson of @ref{1.1}, with the
fractional step carried as an integer numerator over a stated power of two;
the lazy structure of @code{integral} and @code{solve} is unchanged, and the
corpus program records the fixed-point value the solution reaches.

